Fibromyalgia Primary Central Sensitisation
Complex Fatigue Entities
Chronic widespread allodynia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-017
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable widespread pain and severe cognitive dysfunction preventing self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe unremitting pain across all four bodily quadrants, complete insomnia for 48 hours, and confusion. Immediate physical assessment was required to exclude acute systemic infection or metabolic encephalopathy, and to provide central nervous system modulation to manage extreme distress prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin hurts to touch, my brain is completely blank, and I haven't slept in two days because of the pain.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe exacerbation of known primary centralized pain. Over the past 48 hours, they have experienced explosive activation of bilateral pain pathways, leaving them unable to tolerate the weight of clothing. Concurrently, they report totally unrefreshing sleep and profound fibro-fog, manifesting as inability to recall basic timelines or find words.
Functional Status: Bedbound, reliant on flatmate for oral hydration and communication.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation compounding cognitive impairment; high risk of accidental falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Fatigued, lethargic, speech is slow and tangential, tearful.
Physical Exam: Chronic widespread allodynia is clinically evident. Exquisite tenderness to mild palpation across bilateral trapezius, lateral epicondyles, and medial fat pads of the knees. No joint effusions, no erythema. Cranial nerves intact. Alert and oriented to person and place, but exhibits significant delay in cognitive processing.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment, but patient is highly disorganized.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient forgot if evening doses of regular paracetamol were taken due to cognitive fog; flatmate confirms none taken in the last 8 hours.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fibromyalgia Primary Central Sensitisation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute viral encephalitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal pupil responses, no nuchal rigidity, and long-standing history of identical, albeit milder, cognitive symptoms.

Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal temperature, normal respiratory rate, and adequate peripheral perfusion.

Severe major depressive episode - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of stark, physical, reproducible widespread allodynia driving the cognitive collapse, rather than primary mood pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of severe cognitive slowing and diffuse pain mandated urgent face-to-face assessment to rule out life-threatening central nervous system infections or acute metabolic derangements. After confirming the absence of acute systemic illness, it was evident the patient was in a state of severe neuro-inflammatory overdrive driven by sleep deprivation and centralized pain. Immediate administration of a sedating neuro-modulator was required to induce sleep and break the central sensitization cycle, avoiding an inappropriate emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Amitriptyline 25mg orally to induce sleep architecture and modulate neuropathic pain pathways. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, LDN, and PEA.
Positive Behavioral Change: By maintaining a strict 10:00 PM bedtime routine without any screen exposure, you will physically reset your brain's sleep-wake cycle, directly lowering central pain amplification and rewarding yourself with clearer morning cognition.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to initiate definitive central neuro-modulation therapy (e.g., LDN).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient becomes completely unrousable, develops a high fever, or exhibits sudden severe neck stiffness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Fibromyalgia is a disease of central nervous system amplification; the severity of pain does not equal structural bodily damage.
Clinical Course: The acute hyperalgesic state will diminish once restorative sleep is achieved. Cognitive fog will clear gradually over 48-72 hours as the nervous system rests.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Flatmate to call 000 if the patient cannot be woken up, develops a stiff neck, or starts vomiting uncontrollably.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain's pain filter has temporarily crashed due to lack of sleep; the prescribed medication forces the nervous system to shut down and reboot, restoring your ability to filter out normal physical sensations.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to force cognitive tasks tomorrow; completely resting your mind prevents secondary frustration and nervous system wind-up, reinforcing a faster return to clear thinking.

